title: Replace secp256k1/libsecp256k1 with k256 in sp-io and sp-core

doc:
  - audience: [Node Dev, Runtime Dev]
    description: |
      The `secp256k1_ecdsa_recover`/`secp256k1_ecdsa_recover_compressed` host functions
      (both versions) and `sp_core::ecdsa::Pair::verify_deprecated` are now implemented
      with the pure-Rust `k256` crate, completing the migration started when `sp-core`'s
      ECDSA moved to k256. The bitcoin-core `secp256k1` bindings and the pure-Rust
      `libsecp256k1` crate are no longer production dependencies of `sp-io` and
      `sp-core`; they remain as dev-dependencies backing differential tests that pin the
      new implementations to the historical backends bit-for-bit.

      The host function ABI is unchanged: version 1 keeps the legacy "overflowing"
      signature parsing (r/s values above the curve order are reduced modulo the order),
      version 2 keeps strict parsing (such values are rejected as `BadRS`), both keep
      accepting high-S signatures during recovery, and every error variant is preserved
      exactly, so block re-execution of historical chains is unaffected.

      `sp-io` no longer exposes the implicit `secp256k1` cargo feature. Since recovery
      now runs on k256 (which is slower than the C library), chains making heavy use of
      ECDSA recovery in weight-sensitive paths should re-run their benchmarks.

crates:
  - name: sp-io
    bump: major
  - name: sp-core
    bump: patch
